# What is a QALY really claiming about you?
next: {"title":"How can you possibly measure how bad an illness feels? A map of the methods","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/how-can-you-possibly-measure-how-bad-an.cast"}

## Ten Years in a Wheelchair
voice: female
level: basic
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 12
    'y':
      - -0.4
      - 1.2
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 140
      w: 900
      h: 600

Every yes and no from Beslutningsforum rests on one number — so across four parts we take the QALY apart and ask what it quietly claims about your life.
    axes ax x_label "Time (years)" y_label "Health utility  u"

It is built on a ruler with two anchors. Up here, u equals one: a year lived in full health.
    curve line_full dashed #8f887c expr "1"
    label label_full "u = 1  full health" above #8f887c attach_to line_full

    pause 1

Down here, u equals zero — which does not mean no symptoms, it means being dead.
    curve line_dead dashed #8f887c expr "0"
    label label_dead "u = 0  dead" below-left #8f887c attach_to line_dead

    pause 1

And zero is not the floor: states people judge worse than being dead are allowed to take negative values.
    arrow neg_arrow #8a5fa8 from.x 11 from.y -0.02 to.x 11 to.y -0.33
    label label_neg "worse than dead: u < 0" left #8a5fa8 attach_to neg_arrow

    pause 1

Every health state a person can be in gets one number on this ruler. So how does ten years in a wheelchair become a single number?
    highlight line_full line_dead effect glow

    pause 1

Say that state has been valued at zero point six, and the person lives ten years in it.
    curve wheel #2f6b8f expr "0.6" x_from 0 x_to 10
    label label_wheel "u = 0.6" below #2f6b8f attach_to wheel

    pause 1

The QALY is simply the area under that line — quality multiplied by time.
    region block_a x_from 0 x_to 10 between ["wheel","line_dead"] style.fill #87a878

    pause 1

Ten years, each counted as six tenths of a healthy year, add up to six QALYs.
    label label_a "10 years × 0.6 = 6 QALYs" above attach_to block_a

    pause 1

Now hold on to that six, and reshape it.
    erase block_a wheel label_wheel label_a

    pause 0.5

Six years in perfect health — taller, and shorter — covers exactly the same area.
    curve full_seg #b5482e expr "1" x_from 0 x_to 6
    region block_b x_from 0 x_to 6 between ["full_seg","line_dead"] style.fill #87a878

    pause 1

Six QALYs either way.
    label verdict "6 years × 1.0 = 6 QALYs" right attach_to block_b

That is the claim: ten years using a wheelchair counts the same as six years of full health followed by nothing.
    mark mark target verdict kind box
    delivery grave

    pause 1

Nobody ever observed that equality — it is an assumption smuggled in by the multiplication.
    highlight block_b effect glow

It is also the trick that lets nusinersen for spinal muscular atrophy and a routine hip replacement be weighed on one single ruler.
    highlight line_full line_dead effect pulse

So: a life becomes one number by multiplying quality by time — and whether you would accept that trade for yourself is what the rest of this series asks.
    delivery soft

## Building the Number
voice: female
level: basic
use: qaly_profiles
with:
    x_label: Age (years)
    y_label: Health utility u
    x_max: 26
    profiles:
      - {id: current, label: Current care (0), waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.18}, {t: 0.5, u: 0.12}, {t: 1.8, u: 0.08}], death_at: 2}
      - {id: nusi, label: With nusinersen (1), waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.18}, {t: 0.5, u: 0.15}, {t: 0.6, u: 0.42, step: true}, {t: 5, u: 0.45}, {t: 20, u: 0.42}], death_at: 24}
    shade_between:
      a: nusi
      b: current
      gain_label: ΔE
      loss_label: ''

    text calc_head "E = Σ u_t × time_t" x 500 y 650 font_size 36 hidden true
    text line_e0 "Current care:  0.13 × 2 years ≈ 0.3 QALYs" x 500 y 550 font_size 28 hidden true
    text line_e1 "Nusinersen:  0.42 × 24 years ≈ 10 QALYs" x 500 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    text line_disc "At r = 4%: 24 years count as 15.2  →  E₁ ≈ 6.4" #8f887c x 500 y 390 font_size 28 hidden true
    text line_de "ΔE = E₁ − E₀ ≈ 6 QALYs" #b5482e x 500 y 280 font_size 38 hidden true
    mark mark_de target line_de kind box hidden true
    text hip_head "QALYs bought per million kroner" x 500 y 640 font_size 34 hidden true
    text hip_label "Hip replacement  ≈ 20 QALYs" #2f6b8f x 500 y 560 font_size 28 hidden true
    path bar_hip closed #3d3833 points [[260,470],[760,470],[760,520],[260,520]] style.fill #87a878 hidden true
    text nusi_label "Nusinersen at list price  < 1 QALY" #b5482e x 500 y 435 font_size 28 hidden true
    path bar_nusi closed #3d3833 points [[260,350],[276,350],[276,400],[260,400]] style.fill #c96567 hidden true

Now that we have seen how ten years in a wheelchair becomes one number, let us build that number for a decision Beslutningsforum actually had to make: nusinersen for spinal muscular atrophy.
    draw axes full_health_line

    pause 1

Current care, option zero: an infant with severe spinal muscular atrophy, a utility around one tenth of full health, and life measured in a couple of years.
    draw curve_current label_current

    pause 1

With nusinersen, option one, the health state lifts to roughly zero point four, and the life stretches on for decades.
    draw curve_nusi label_nusi

    pause 1

The green area between the two curves is everything the drug adds — height times width, quality times time.
    draw gain_regions label_gain

    pause 1

And it is won two ways at once: a little more quality in each year, and many more years to have it in.
    focus gain_regions

    pause 1

    clear

An area on a chart is not yet a number, so we count it: the utility in each year, multiplied by that year, added up.
    draw calc_head

    pause 1

Current care buys about zero point one three of full health for two years — roughly zero point three QALYs.
    draw line_e0

    pause 1

Nusinersen buys about zero point four two for twenty-four years — call it ten QALYs, before discounting.
    draw line_e1

    pause 1

But the Norwegian guideline discounts at four percent, so health far in the future counts for less today, and twenty-four years shrink to about fifteen — E one falls to six point four.
    draw line_disc

    pause 1

A third of this child's health disappeared into the discount rate, without anyone making a clinical claim at all.
    highlight line_disc effect glow

    pause 1

Subtract, and a whole childhood becomes one figure: delta E, about six QALYs.
    draw line_de mark_de delivery grave

    pause 1

    clear

Six QALYs sounds large — until you ask what else six QALYs would have cost.
    draw hip_head

    pause 1

A hip replacement runs to something like a hundred thousand kroner for a couple of QALYs, so a million kroner buys roughly twenty of them.
    draw bar_hip hip_label

    pause 1

Nusinersen at list price runs to millions of kroner per QALY, so the same million buys less than one.
    draw bar_nusi nusi_label

That sliver is not a drawing error — it is the same money, drawn on the same scale.
    highlight bar_nusi effect glow

    pause 1

These are orders of magnitude rather than exact figures, and Beslutningsforum's eventual yes came with a confidential price and an age limit, not at the list price on this chart.
    text scale_note "Order of magnitude, same scale, same krone." #8f887c x 500 y 270 font_size 24

So a QALY claims this much about you: your health is a height, your life is a width, and their product is comparable to everyone else's — which is exactly what makes the boring hip replacement so hard to argue with.

## The trade hidden in the multiplication
voice: female
level: basic
use: qaly_profiles
with:
    x_label: Years from now
    y_label: Health utility (u)
    x_max: 12
    profiles:
      - {id: chair, label: Wheelchair — 6 QALYs, color: '#2f6b8f', waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.6}, {t: 10, u: 0.6}], death_at: 10}
      - {id: short, label: Six full years — 6 QALYs, color: '#b5482e', waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 1}, {t: 6, u: 1}], death_at: 6}
      - {id: decline, label: 'Good, then poor — 6 QALYs', color: '#8a5fa8', waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.9}, {t: 4, u: 0.9}, {t: 4, u: 0.4, step: true}, {t: 10, u: 0.4}], death_at: 10}
    shade_between: null
sources:
    - id: pliskin1980
      title: Utility functions for life years and health status
      authors: Pliskin, Shepard and Weinstein
      year: 1980
      finding: Conditions under which QALYs are a valid utility measure

Now that we have seen how the number is built, watch what the multiplication itself quietly claims — because that claim is about you, and it decides which drugs Beslutningsforum buys.
    draw axes full_health_line

Ten years in a wheelchair, weighted 0.6: ten times zero point six, six QALYs.
    draw curve_chair label_chair

    pause 1

And here is what those same six QALYs also buy — six years in perfect health, then nothing.
    draw curve_short label_short

    pause 1

The arithmetic holds these two lives exactly equal. Would you hand over four years of your life to leave the wheelchair?
    point at.ref curve_short gesture circle

    pause 1

A third life: four years at 0.9, then six at 0.4 — three point six plus two point four, six again.
    draw curve_decline label_decline

Three quite different lives, and the number cannot tell them apart.
    focus curve_chair curve_short curve_decline

    pause 1

Equating the first two assumes a constant proportional trade-off: the share of remaining life you'd trade for health doesn't depend on how much life is on the table.
    focus curve_chair curve_short

Adding the years up assumes order is irrelevant — good health early or late, a slow decline or a steady state, only the sum survives.
    focus curve_decline

And that single weight, 0.6, stands in for everyone in the state, whatever a particular person would say about their own wheelchair.
    highlight label_chair effect pulse

In 1980, Pliskin, Shepard and Weinstein wrote down exactly which conditions your preferences must satisfy for these sums to be a valid utility measure — constant proportional trade-off among them.
    delivery soft

Which makes it testable. So do real people trade the way the formula says?
    clear

    path panel_axes points [[200,620],[200,180],[880,180]]
    text cap_x "Years spent in the health state" x 540 y 135 font_size 22
    text cap_y "Share of remaining life you would give up" x 400 y 660 font_size 22

Constant proportional trade-off predicts this flat line: the same share of remaining life surrendered, whatever the duration.
    path flat_line dashed #2f6b8f cites ["pliskin1980"] points [[215,430],[870,430]]
    label label_flat "What the multiplication assumes" below #2f6b8f attach_to flat_line

    pause 1

Time trade-off studies keep finding this instead — over a short horizon people will barely trade at all, and the share grows as the horizon lengthens.
    path data_curve #b5482e points [[215,235],[300,300],[400,365],[520,412],[640,442],[760,458],[870,468]]
    label label_data "What people actually answer" above #b5482e attach_to data_curve

    pause 1

Where the lines part, the assumption is failing — and it fails hardest for short horizons, exactly where a drug like nusinersen is judged.
    point at.x 300 at.y 365 gesture circle

In the very worst states some people answer that more time is worse than less — something the multiplication cannot express at all.
    mark mark target label_data kind box

So: would you take that trade? Most people, asked directly, will not — which is why the QALY is not a discovery about you, but a convention, with its assumptions written down where they can be argued with.
    delivery grave

## Why One Currency at All
voice: female
level: basic

Now that we have seen the trade hidden inside quality times time, one question is left: why force everything into a single number at all?
    text head_a "Nusinersen for SMA" #b5482e x 270 y 665 font_size 28

Nusinersen's effect in spinal muscular atrophy arrives as a list: milestones reached, months off a ventilator, a child sitting unsupported at two.
    text a1 "Motor milestones gained" x 270 y 595 font_size 22
    text a2 "Months free of a ventilator" x 270 y 548 font_size 22
    text a3 "Sitting unsupported at age two" x 270 y 501 font_size 22

    pause 1

A hip replacement arrives as a different list: four points less pain, three hundred metres more walking, back at work by spring.
    text head_b "Hip replacement" #2f6b8f x 740 y 665 font_size 28
    text b1 "Pain score down four points" x 740 y 595 font_size 22
    text b2 "Walking 300 metres further" x 740 y 548 font_size 22
    text b3 "Back at work in three months" x 740 y 501 font_size 22

    pause 1

Now subtract one list from the other — there is no operation that does it, because the two share no unit.
    text qmark "?" #8a5fa8 x 505 y 548 font_size 64

    pause 1

Yet one budget must choose between them, and without a shared unit the choice falls to whoever argues most persuasively in the room.
    text qline "Which list is worth more?" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 400 font_size 26
    delivery grave

    pause 1

So we impose a unit: every outcome in either list is re-expressed as utility multiplied by time.
    clear keep ["head_a","head_b"]

Three quality-adjusted life years for the child; about one and a half for the hip, on illustrative round numbers.
    text note "illustrative round numbers" #8f887c x 500 y 706 font_size 18
    text e_a "ΔE ≈ 3.0 QALYs" #b5482e x 270 y 580 font_size 26
    text e_b "ΔE ≈ 1.5 QALYs" #2f6b8f x 740 y 580 font_size 26

    pause 1

Their incremental costs, delta C, differ by nearly two orders of magnitude.
    text c_a "ΔC ≈ 6 000 000 kr" #b5482e x 270 y 515 font_size 26
    text c_b "ΔC ≈ 90 000 kr" #2f6b8f x 740 y 515 font_size 26

Divide cost by effect and each becomes a price per QALY: two million kroner against sixty thousand.
    text icer_a "ICER ≈ 2 000 000 kr / QALY" x 270 y 450 font_size 24
    text icer_b "ICER ≈ 60 000 kr / QALY" x 740 y 450 font_size 24

    pause 1

Read it the other way round — the same million kroner buys half a healthy year here, and seventeen there.
    text mline "The same 1 000 000 kr buys:" x 500 y 350 font_size 26
    text buy_a "0.5 QALYs" #b5482e x 270 y 270 font_size 36
    text buy_b "17 QALYs" #2f6b8f x 740 y 270 font_size 36

That is opportunity cost made visible: the patients you never meet, finally on the same page as the ones you do.
    mark mark_b target buy_b kind box

This is what the common currency buys us — nytte, benefit, made comparable across diseases that have nothing else in common.
    highlight e_a e_b effect glow

    pause 1

But a currency charges its own exchange fee.
    clear

The number cannot say who gained: three QALYs for an infant and three for a pensioner are the same three.
    text t3 "What the single number cannot say" x 500 y 620 font_size 30
    text p1 "Who gained — a child, or a pensioner" x 500 y 530 font_size 24

It cannot say how bad they were to begin with — alvorlighet, severity, has to be added back afterwards as a separate criterion, which is why nusinersen was first refused and later accepted with an age limit rather than by its ICER alone.
    text p2 "How bad they were to begin with — alvorlighet, severity" x 500 y 470 font_size 24

And it cannot say whose values fixed u in the first place, which is exactly where we go next.
    text p3 "Whose values fixed u in the first place" x 500 y 410 font_size 24

    pause 1

Across these four parts: ten years in a wheelchair became a utility, utility times time became a QALY, the multiplication hid a trade you might not accept for yourself — and this one number is what lets a health system see everyone it could have helped instead.
    text final "One currency makes comparison possible — not automatically right." x 500 y 300 font_size 24
    mark mark_final target final kind box
    delivery grave

